"""answers"""

"""1. Why do you think companies analyze large volumes of data?"""
"""lets say i am a seller, so i want to know what my customers like and dislike, so i can improve my products, and also to know what my competitors are doing, what they are selling, and how they selling it, these were just few examples, there are bunch of examples, that's why companies analyze large volumes of data, to improve their products and services"""

"""2. If analyzing and sorting large data manually in Excel is difficult, how do you think Python can help solve this problem?"""
"""if you try to open a huge file in excel, it starts lagging or even crashes, and doing things by hand takes forever. with python, you just write a few lines of code and it sorts, filters, and calculates millions of rows in seconds. plus, once you write the code, you can reuse it every day without doing the same boring work again and again"""

"""3. Imagine you work at a sales company that receives data about 10,000 customer transactions daily. How would you analyze this data?"""
"""first, i would load the daily data into python using a library like pandas and clean up any missing or broken info. then i would check the main things: what are the most popular products today, what time do people buy the most, and how much total money we made. after that, i would compare today's numbers with yesterday's or last week's to see if our sales are growing or dropping"""

"""4. In your opinion, what tasks can Python be useful for in BI processes? (e.g., data cleaning, visualization, etc.)"""
"""python is basically like a swiss army knife for BI. first of all, data cleaning—fixing messy tables, removing duplicates, and filling in missing values. second, visualization—making clear charts and graphs so bosses can easily understand the numbers. third, automation—setting up scripts that automatically grab data and make reports every morning. and even predicting future sales using past data"""

"""5. If you wanted to compare a company's profit year by year, how could this be done using Python?"""
"""it is actually pretty simple. i would load all the sales and expenses data into python, group the data by year, and calculate the total profit for each year. then, i would use a charting library like matplotlib or seaborn to draw a bar chart or a line graph. that way, you can clearly see at a glance which years were great and where the profit went up or down"""

"""6. If you don't know Python, what difficulties might you face when working with large datasets?"""
"""without python, you are stuck doing a lot of manual work. standard tools like excel have row limits and freeze when the file is too big. you would waste hours copying and pasting things, which also means you are much more likely to make human mistakes. basically, tasks that take 5 seconds in python could take you a whole week to finish by hand"""